<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  A safety page that only says “check the stars” leaves the useful part out. Stars, crash-test scores, and recall campaigns answer different questions, they come from different organizations, and none of them tells you whether the specific car in a driveway has been repaired. This hub is the map for that work: which rating to open first, what a recall legally requires, and where a VIN lookup fits when the brochure and the car disagree.
</p>

<h2>Two rating systems, and why they disagree</h2>
<p>
  The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration publishes a 5-star rating for many new vehicles. The program is a comparative crash-test score for that model year, not a promise about a used car’s condition. NHTSA’s public ratings live at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/ratings" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/ratings</a>. Our walkthrough of how those stars are built, including what the frontal, side, and rollover tests actually measure, is <a href="/guides/safety/nhtsa-ratings-explained/">NHTSA safety ratings explained</a>.
</p>
<p>
  The Insurance Institute for Highway Safety runs a separate program. IIHS ratings (Good, Acceptable, Marginal, Poor, plus the Top Safety Pick designations) come from its own crash tests and from headlight and crash-avoidance evaluations. They are not a reskin of NHTSA’s stars. A vehicle can look strong on one site and ordinary on the other because the tests are not the same tests. Read both before you treat a single badge in an ad as the whole record. IIHS publishes its results at <a href="https://www.iihs.org/ratings" rel="noopener">iihs.org/ratings</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Ratings are assigned to a vehicle as it was tested, usually a particular body style and sometimes a particular build configuration. A later facelift, a missing option, or a different body can sit outside the test that produced the badge on the window sticker. When you are looking at a used car, write down the model year from the VIN, not from the seller’s title line, and match that year on both sites.
</p>

<h2>What a star rating does not cover</h2>
<p>
  A crash-test rating does not say whether this car has an open recall, a branded title, or a repair that was done badly. It also does not age with the car. Airbags, seatbelts, and electronic stability control are part of why a newer design scores well, and they are also the parts that show up in safety recalls years after the test. The rating and the recall list have to be read together.
</p>
<p>
  Advanced driver-assistance features — automatic emergency braking, blind-spot warning, lane keeping — are uneven. Some are tested by IIHS as crash-prevention systems. Many are optional, named differently by each manufacturer, and easy to mis-list in a classified ad. A VIN decode from NHTSA’s product catalog returns the equipment the manufacturer reported for that vehicle. It is a better starting point than the seller’s bullet list. Use the <a href="/vin-decoder/">VIN decoder</a> and then confirm anything safety-critical on a test drive, because a feature code does not prove the camera or radar still works.
</p>
<p>
  Child-seat fit, headlight aim, and tire condition never appear in a model-year star. Those are inspection items. If you are buying used, the practical list is the <a href="/guides/buying/used-car-checklist/">used-car checklist</a> and the longer <a href="/guides/how-to-check-car-before-buying/">pre-purchase guide</a>. Safety research tells you which design to shop. The inspection tells you whether this example of that design is intact.
</p>

<h2>Recalls are a repair obligation, not a rating</h2>
<p>
  A safety recall means the manufacturer or NHTSA has decided that a vehicle or a piece of equipment has a defect that creates an unreasonable safety risk, or that it fails to meet a federal motor vehicle safety standard. The duty to provide a free remedy comes from federal law, 49 U.S.C. Chapter 301. NHTSA’s recall search is at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>. Our <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a> queries the same public database by VIN.
</p>
<p>
  The remedy is performed by a franchised dealer of that make, at no charge for parts and labor, regardless of the car’s age or mileage. There is no general expiration date on a motor-vehicle safety recall. If you already paid to fix the same defect before the campaign opened, ask the manufacturer about reimbursement. The dealer that sold you the car does not have to be the dealer that does the repair.
</p>
<p>
  A recall campaign is often limited to a build range, a plant, or a component supplier lot. That is why a page about “the 2018 model” cannot honestly print a single recall count and call it yours. Two cars of the same year can have different open campaigns. The VIN is the lookup key. Campaign numbers, component names, and the manufacturer’s remedy text come back from NHTSA. We do not invent those.
</p>
<p>
  An open recall is not the same thing as a lemon-law claim. The recall is a free safety repair. A lemon-law claim is a state consumer action about a defect the manufacturer has failed to fix under the warranty. If the same problem keeps returning after the recall repair, read the <a href="/guides/lemon-law/">lemon-law guide</a> and the <a href="/guides/lemon-law/federal-lemon-law/">federal warranty guide</a>, then confirm the repair-attempt rule with your state attorney general. Do not treat a round number you saw on a forum as the statute.
</p>
<p>
  Owners also miss recall mail because the registration address is old. Running a check when you buy, and again a couple of times a year, catches campaigns that opened after the last letter. How an open campaign can affect a later sale is covered in <a href="/guides/recalls-resale-value/">recalls and resale value</a>. The short version is that a buyer who looks the VIN up will see it, and a completed repair with a dealer invoice is easier to explain than a promise that “it was probably done.”
</p>

<h2>A workable order of operations</h2>
<p>
  When you are comparing new cars, pull the NHTSA stars and the IIHS ratings for the exact model year and body style, then read the footnotes on which trims were tested. When you are comparing used cars, do that first so you are not shopping a design you already dislike, then decode the VIN, then run the recall check, then pay for a history report if the price justifies it. A free decode does not include accident or title-brand history. That distinction is spelled out in <a href="/guides/carfax-vs-vin-lookup/">Carfax versus a free VIN lookup</a>.
</p>
<p>
  If the listing’s year, make, or model does not match the decode, stop. VIN cloning and title games are described in <a href="/guides/how-vin-fraud-works/">how VIN fraud works</a> and in the <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/">salvage-title guide</a>. A clean crash-test score on the model does not rehabilitate a car whose identity does not match its paperwork.
</p>
<p>
  Complaints that have not yet become a recall still matter. NHTSA accepts vehicle-owner complaints and uses them, along with crash data and manufacturer reports, to decide whether to open an investigation. Filing a complaint is not the same as scheduling a repair, but it is how a pattern becomes visible. The complaint form is on NHTSA’s site, linked from the recalls pages.
</p>

